# EP01 — How to Survive Krampus (According to Folklore)
# id | picture | tokens | caption | narration      (ward lines: id | picture | ward ... | NUMERAL | rule | narration)
# Sources: every factual line is tagged in scripts/ep01_krampus.md. Source list (used by upload_kit.py):
# [S1] Wikipedia, "Krampus": Alpine range, Krampusnacht on 5 December, name from German Krampen ("claw"), horns, tongue, chains, bells, birch ruten, the basket, the pairing with St Nicholas, Krampuslauf, Krampuskarten, the post-1932 Dollfuss-era prohibition, golden ruten kept on display. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Krampus
# [S2] Wikipedia, "Saint Nicholas Day": 6 December feast; the saint's eve visits in Austria, Bavaria and neighbours, gifts for good children. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Nicholas_Day
# [S3] Maurice Bruce, "The Krampus in Styria", Folklore 69:1 (1958), 44-47: argues for pre-Christian roots and describes the Styrian visits with the rod. https://doi.org/10.1080/0015587X.1958.9717121
# [S4] National Geographic, "Who Is Krampus? Explaining the Horrific Christmas Beast" (2013): Krampusnacht, Krampuslauf runs fuelled by schnapps, greeting cards from the 1800s, the 1930s suppression. https://www.nationalgeographic.com/culture/article/131217-krampus-christmas-santa-devil
# [S5] University Observer, "Krampus: Demons, Austrian Fascists, and the Death of Culture": the Fatherland Front's hostility and the 1934 New York Times report "Krampus Disliked in Fascist Austria". https://universityobserver.ie/krampus-demons-austrian-fascists-and-death-culture/
# [S6] Austria.info (Austrian National Tourist Office), "A Raucous Christmas: Winter Traditions in Austria": Krampus and Perchten runs today. https://www.austria.info/en-us/inspiration/raucous-traditions
# [S7] Al Ridenour, "The Krampus and the Old, Dark Christmas" (Feral House, 2016): Nicholas plays, house visits, and the modern revival.

# ---- HOOK ----
k01 | a small alpine mountain village at night, wooden chalets with lit windows, deep snow, a church steeple, wide scene | snow night zoomin | Fifth of December | It is the fifth of December, somewhere high in the Alps. The sun went down at half past four, and every door in the village is already bolted.
k02 | a terrified child in a nightshirt peeking around a wooden door, holding a candle, full figure | shiver night | | You're seven years old. And out in the street, you can hear it. Bells. Heavy iron bells. And under the bells, the drag of a chain on frozen stone.
k03 | Krampus, a tall hairy horned goat demon with cloven hooves and a long red tongue, holding a bundle of birch rods and an iron chain, full figure | loom night snow | | Something with goat horns, black fur and a tongue as long as your arm is walking from house to house. It carries a bundle of birch sticks. And on its back there's a basket, big enough for a child.
k04 | =k02 | zoomin night | | Tonight, it's looking for you. Not for being evil. For being naughty. And the old stories are very clear about what happens to naughty children who end up in that basket.
k05 | - | quote | Some say he drowns them. Some say he eats them. Some say he carries them straight to Hell. — the Alpine tellings | Some say he drowns them. Some say he eats them. Some say he carries them straight down to Hell.
k06 | =k03 | zoomout | | But here's the thing. Alpine families have survived this night for hundreds of years. They have rules. Five of them. Follow them, and you'll see the sixth of December. Break one, and, well. Let's find out.
k07 | an open old leather-bound book with blank pages and a melting candle beside it | title dust | Krampus | This is the Ward Book. And tonight, how to survive Krampus.

# ---- WHO IS KRAMPUS ----
k08 | a map-like woodcut of snowy alpine mountain peaks, wide scene | panr dust | The Eastern Alps | First, know your enemy. Krampus belongs to the Eastern Alps. Austria, Bavaria, South Tyrol, and the mountain valleys around them, where winter nights are long and very, very dark.
k09 | a large clawed monster hand with long curved nails, close up | pulse | Krampen = claw | His name probably comes from the old German word Krampen, which means claw. Which tells you a lot about how this relationship is going to go.
k10 | =k03 | zoomin | | He's usually drawn half goat, half demon. Horns. Hooves. Shaggy fur. A long, pointed, ridiculous tongue. Chains around his body, and bells that you hear long before you see him.
k11 | a bundle of birch twigs tied together with red ribbon, the rute | sway | the Rute | And he carries a rute. A bundle of birch branches, for swatting children. Some families say it's a warning. Some say it's a punishment. The children say it's both.
k12 | a woven wicker basket on leather straps with small legs sticking out of it | bob | | But the rod is not the worst part. The worst part is the basket on his back. That's the exit for kids who are beyond saving.
k13 | Saint Nicholas, an old bearded bishop with a tall mitre and a crozier staff, full figure | breathe dust | Saint Nicholas | Now here's the strangest part of the whole legend. Krampus never travels alone. He works for a saint.
k14 | =k13 | zoomin | Feast day: 6 December | On the sixth of December, the Church celebrates Saint Nicholas, the kind bishop who brings presents. And on the eve of his feast, the fifth, Nicholas goes from house to house. Good children get apples, nuts and sweets.
k15 | Saint Nicholas standing beside a horned hairy Krampus holding chains, both full figure, side by side | breathe | | And the naughty ones? That's what the assistant is for. The saint does the rewards. The demon does the paperwork.

# ---- WARD I ----
k16 | an old hourglass with a tiny horned devil perched on top of it | ward pulse | I | Know which night it is | Ward one. Know which night it is.
k17 | =k01 | panl snow night | | Krampusnacht, Krampus Night, is the fifth of December. That's his night. Not Christmas Eve, not Halloween. The fifth. Everywhere else in the world, this is a normal Saturday. In the Alps, it's the night the doors get bolted.
k18 | a small child at a window watching snow fall, seen from inside, full figure | breathe snow | | So the first rule is simply this. If you're anywhere in the Eastern Alps in early December, check the calendar. If it says five, be indoors early, be nice to your siblings, and for heaven's sake, eat your vegetables.

# ---- WARD II ----
k19 | a heavy iron chain lying coiled on the ground with an open iron shackle | ward | II | Never meet him without the saint | Ward two. Never meet Krampus without the saint.
k20 | =k15 | zoomin | | In the old house visits, the two arrive together. Nicholas walks in first, calm and holy. Krampus comes in behind him, rattling, growling, and very much on a leash.
k21 | =k19 | pulse | Binding the Devil | And look at those chains. Many folklorists read them as a symbol of the Christian Church binding the Devil. Krampus is dangerous, yes. But he's a chained danger. He goes where the saint lets him go.
k22 | =k13 | zoomout | | So as long as Saint Nicholas is in the room, you are, technically, under protection. The danger is the stories where Krampus turns up first. Alone. Without his boss.

# ---- WARD III ----
k23 | a small child kneeling with hands pressed together in prayer, full figure | ward breathe | III | Have your answer ready | Ward three. Have your answer ready.
k24 | =k13 | zoomin | | When the saint arrives, he asks questions. Have you been good this year? Do you know your prayers? Have you listened to your parents? In some families, the bishop even reads from a big golden book of everything you did.
k25 | =k23 | hold | | Children were expected to say a prayer, or a poem, or at least promise to behave. A good answer means presents. A bad answer means the saint steps aside, and lets the assistant have a word.
k26 | =k15 | zoomin | | So rehearse. Out loud. In front of a mirror. You are about to have the most important job interview of your life, and the interviewer has horns.

# ---- WARD IV ----
k27 | a golden painted bundle of birch twigs hanging on a wooden wall from a nail | ward sway | IV | Keep the golden rod on the wall | Ward four. Keep the golden rod on the wall.
k28 | =k27 | zoomin | | Here's a detail most people never hear. In some Alpine homes, the birch rute isn't thrown away after Krampus Night. It gets painted gold and hung up in the house, where it stays all year.
k29 | a mother pointing at a golden rod on the wall while a small child looks up nervously, full figures | breathe | | Why? It's a reminder. Every time you misbehave in, say, July, somebody points at the wall. No words needed. The demon may be gone, but his invoice is still hanging in the kitchen.
k30 | =k12 | bob | | Think of it as a ward that works in reverse. It doesn't keep Krampus out. It keeps you from ever needing that basket.

# ---- WARD V ----
k31 | a crowd of costumed Krampus runners with huge carved wooden masks, horns and bells, running down a snowy street at night, wide scene | ward wind | V | On the night of the run, stay off the street | Ward five. On the night of the run, stay off the street.
k32 | =k31 | panr snow night | Krampuslauf | Today, in many Alpine towns, Krampus doesn't just visit houses. He runs. The Krampuslauf, the Krampus run, is a parade of young men in fur suits and hand-carved wooden masks, charging through the streets with bells and torches.
k33 | a carved wooden Krampus mask with horns and fangs, hanging on a wall | pulse | | Some of those masks take weeks to carve and weigh several kilos. The suits are made of real goat or sheep fur. And quite a few of the runners have had a few glasses of schnapps to keep warm.
k34 | =k02 | shiver | | And the rods are real. Runners swat at the crowd, usually on the legs. Locals know to stand back, or to stand behind someone taller. Tourists learn the hard way.
k35 | =k31 | zoomout | | So the final ward is the most modern one. Krampus Night is a party now. But it's a party where the guests carry sticks.

# ---- WHERE HE REALLY COMES FROM ----
k36 | an ancient stone standing in a snowy mountain valley under a crescent moon, wide scene | fog night zoomin | Older than the saint? | So where did Krampus really come from? This is where it gets interesting, because nobody is completely sure.
k37 | an old scholar with a quill writing at a desk by candlelight, full figure | breathe dust | Maurice Bruce, 1958 | In nineteen fifty-eight, the folklorist Maurice Bruce wrote that Krampus in Styria, a region of Austria, looked much older than Christianity. A horned winter spirit, from the days when mid-winter belonged to the dark.
k38 | a medieval stage play with a bishop actor and a horned devil actor in costume on a wooden stage, wide scene | panl | The Nicholas plays | Others are more careful. The clearer evidence comes from Christian Nicholas plays and house visits, where a devil character follows the saint around. Krampus may be an ancient spirit that got baptised. Or a stage devil who got very, very popular.
k39 | =k15 | zoomin | | Either way, the partnership stuck. A kind saint and a terrifying servant. Reward and punishment, walking side by side, down every snowy street.

# ---- THE ONE THING KRAMPUS FEARED ----
k40 | a stern politician in a 1930s suit standing at a podium, full figure | breathe | Austria, 1930s | And here's the twist. For centuries, nothing could stop Krampus. Not priests, not parents, not the Enlightenment. And then came the nineteen thirties.
k41 | an official decree document with a large wax seal and a crossed out horned demon drawn on it | pulse | Banned | After nineteen thirty-two, Austria's authoritarian government, under Engelbert Dollfuss, turned against the Krampus tradition. In nineteen thirty-four, the New York Times even reported that Krampus was disliked in Fascist Austria.
k42 | =k03 | loom snow night | | Did it work? Not really. These are remote mountain valleys. You cannot police every village on the fifth of December. Krampus simply went back to doing what he does best. Walking from house to house in the dark.
k43 | an old Victorian greeting postcard showing a smiling Krampus carrying a child in a basket, decorative | breathe | Gruss vom Krampus | Long before that, he'd already conquered the post office. From the eighteen hundreds, Austrians sent Krampus cards. Greetings from Krampus. Horned demons, chasing children, and the message, basically, happy holidays.
k44 | =k31 | panr wind | | And today he's more popular than ever. Krampus runs have spread across Europe and to America, with Hollywood films, beer labels, and costume clubs that spend all year building the scariest suit in the valley.

# ---- THE VERDICT ----
k45 | =k23 | breathe | | So, how do you survive Krampus? Honestly, the folklore's answer is a little annoying. You don't fight him. You don't trap him. You behave.
k46 | =k13 | zoomin | | Krampus was never really a monster you defeat. He's a reminder. A way for long, dark winters to teach children that what you do all year comes due at the end of it.
k47 | =k02 | night | | So, know the night. Stay near the saint. Have your answer ready. Respect the golden rod. And when you hear bells on a December street, take one step back from the kerb.
k48 | =k07 | dust zoomin | | That's five wards, from one of the oldest winter legends in Europe. Next week, we go somewhere much colder, to a monster that isn't sent by a saint, and doesn't care if you've been good.
k49 | a gaunt starving figure made of ice and bone standing in a snowy pine forest, frightening, full figure | endcard snow night | The Wendigo | It's the creature of hunger. The Wendigo. And its rules are very different. Subscribe, so the Ward Book is open when it comes. And until then, stay inside on the fifth.
